CRuby on WASIX as a POSIX platform, starting child processes with
posix_spawn().

CRuby's only WebAssembly port is ruby.wasm's, for WASI preview 1, which
every `defined(__wasm__) && !defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)` selects: setjmp and
Fibers on Binaryen's Asyncify, the collector reading locals through an
Asyncify unwind, and main() run inside an Asyncify loop (rb_wasm_rt_start,
which ruby-<minor>.patch turns off).
WASIX has threads, processes, signals and sockets, and env:ruby's build
keeps the collector's view of locals another way (build.sh), so with
`__wasix__` (wasix-libc's macro, which configure's CPPFLAGS define for
every file) Ruby builds as it does on Linux: libc's setjmp, which the
compiler turns into WebAssembly exceptions, Ruby's own machine-stack
marking, and an ordinary main().

WASIX has no fork(). Ruby without fork() either has spawnv() (Windows),
which redirects descriptors in the parent around the call, or runs
system() and returns a pid of 1 without waiting, which is how a failing
`rake test` passed on ruby.wasm. With RUBY_SPAWN_WITHOUT_FORK (WASIX
without a working fork), rb_spawn_process starts the child with
posix_spawn(): the redirections, closes and working directory a forked
child would have set up become file actions, so the parent's descriptors
and directory never change, and the environment is the envp Ruby already
builds for exec. `system`, `spawn`, backticks, `IO.popen` and so Open3 go
through it, and their children's statuses are waited for as with fork. A
pipe's IO closes and waits for its child as it does with fork. rlimits,
uid and gid are refused with ENOTSUP. `fork` and `IO.popen("-")` raise
NotImplementedError, as everywhere without fork(). A forked child makes its
standard descriptors blocking before exec, since Ruby opens pipes
non-blocking; the descriptors that become the child's 0, 1 and 2 are
made blocking before posix_spawn() the same way, or `cat` reading
Open3's pipe failed with EAGAIN.

`exec` passes the environment as ENV has it too. wasix-libc's execv()
and execl() give the new program the environment the process started
with, so an `exec` after a change to ENV lost it: `bundle exec ruby x.rb`
ran x.rb without the RUBYOPT Bundler had set. On WASIX they are execve()
and execle() with environ when Ruby built no envp of its own, as the
spawn path passes it.

wasix-libc has no flock(), so Ruby compiles missing/flock.c, which on WASI
fails every call with EINVAL: File#flock, and with it the locks RubyGems
and Bundler take, raised. On WASIX it calls the CI runner's
`wasix_32v1.iwci_fd_flock` (vendor/README.md), flock(2) as Linux has it.

ext/socket's Happy Eyeballs code reads rb_fdset_t's `fdset` member, which
only the large-fd_set variant has; without NFDBITS (WASI's headers have
none) rb_fdset_t is a plain fd_set, and rb_fd_term() does nothing. And
its extconf.rb took `msg_control` in struct msghdr to mean control
messages work, which WASI's headers declare with no struct cmsghdr or
CMSG_* macros; it now checks for CMSG_FIRSTHDR too, and without them
BasicSocket#sendmsg and #recvmsg carry no ancillary data.

io/console skips itself on any platform matching /wasi/, which
`wasm32-wasix` does; WASIX has termios, so it is built.

WASIX's poll.h makes POLLPRI another name for POLLIN, so RB_WAITFD_PRI
was RB_WAITFD_IN and every wait for input (IO#wait_readable, and with it
Net::HTTP's reads) asked select() for exceptional conditions too, which
WASIX refuses with ENOSYS. RB_WAITFD_PRI gets a bit of its own there;
nothing passes these values to poll() off Linux.

On Linux a thread blocked in select() or a read is woken by a signal
(ubf_select sends SIGVTALRM), and that is how Thread#kill, Thread#raise,
Timeout and the main thread's exit reach it. WASIX cannot end a blocking
call that way: a signal to another thread neither ends the host's wait
nor, sent to a main thread blocked in poll_oneoff, reaches its handler,
and SIGVTALRM's default ends the process. So USE_UBF_LIST is off, and a
wait on descriptors (thread_pthread.c's native_fd_select, and io.c's
nogvl_wait_for through it) is a series of waits of at most 100 ms
between which the thread looks for an interrupt, returning EINTR for
one as a signal would have.
